Reused evidence:
- REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0052"],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0061"],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

New evidence:
- Social welfare planning and budget expansion were explicit state priorities in early 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt’s Ministry of Planning and Economic Development announces pillars of 21/2022 social welfare plan","publisher":"Ministry of Planning and Economic Development","publication_date":"2022-01-21","url":"https://www.mped.gov.eg/singlenews?id=951&lang=en","claim":"The 21/2022 social welfare plan said social protection would expand to poor and vulnerable groups, with EGP 283.4 billion allocated to social protection and targets including 4 million poor families, 18 million insured workers, and higher pensions.","locator":"lines 61-65","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Government budget-and-target announcement with specific figures and program goals, directly tied to the 2022 fiscal plan.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 fiscal-year plan and targets.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi government through executive planning and budget-setting authority; implementation is shared with ministries and agencies.","contrary_evidence":["This is a plan and budget target, not independent proof that all promised delivery was achieved.","The source is government-produced and may overstate intended benefits."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The government also implemented a major pension increase in March 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"تنفيذا لتوجيهات السيد رئيس الجمهورية التأمينات \"زيادة المعاشات بنسبة 13من اجمالى قيمة المعاش\"","publisher":"National Organization for Social Insurance","publication_date":"2022-03-21","url":"https://www.nosi.gov.eg/ar/News/Pages/21-3-2022.aspx","claim":"The insurance authority said a 13% pension increase would take effect on April 1, 2022, benefiting 10.7 million citizens at a monthly cost of EGP 3.8 billion.","locator":"search result snippet and article header/summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official social-insurance announcement with precise beneficiary counts and fiscal cost, but I could not cleanly extract the page text through the browser.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 welfare action.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the president's directives and implemented through the social-insurance apparatus; the announcement is official but not independently audited here.","contrary_evidence":["A pension boost helps purchasing power but does not by itself prove broad, durable welfare improvement.","Nominal increases can be eroded quickly by inflation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.10"]}

- Health coverage was still being expanded under a presidential directive in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"تكليف رئاسي باستكمال تطبيق التأمين الصحى الشامل رغم التحديات الاقتصادية العالمية","publisher":"Egyptian Tax Authority","publication_date":"2022-05-06","url":"https://eta.gov.eg/ar/news/tklyf-ryasy-bastkmal-ttbyq-altamyn-alshy-alshaml-rghm-althdyat-alaqtsadyt-alalmyt","claim":"The article says there was a presidential directive to continue implementing universal health insurance despite global economic challenges, and to consider extending coverage to more people in need to strengthen social protection.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government notice with a direct presidential directive and concrete policy direction, but the snippet is all I could retrieve cleanly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy direction.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi via a presidential directive; operational rollout still depends on health institutions.","contrary_evidence":["The source shows intent and direction, not completed nationwide coverage in 2022.","The page is a government statement, so effect estimates need independent corroboration."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9"]}

- Decent Life was a large rural service-delivery and living-standards program.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"1st stage of Decent Life initiative to conclude by December","publisher":"Cairo Governorate Electronic Portal","publication_date":"2022-08-02","url":"https://www.cairo.gov.eg/en/news/news-eng/2022/7bdec83e7a524d01b93ec509aaf78ff7","claim":"The Decent Life Initiative, initiated experimentally in 2019 by President Abdel-Fattah El-Sisi, aimed to improve living standards, infrastructure, and basic services including healthcare across the countryside; the project covered 4,658 villages and 58 percent of Egypt’s population.","locator":"lines 272-275","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official portal reproducing a contemporaneous report with specific scale and purpose; the underlying item is attributed to Al-Ahram Online.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 implementation and scale.","ruler_attribution":"High attribution: the initiative is explicitly presidential, though implementation is decentralized across ministries and governorates.","contrary_evidence":["The article describes scope and goals more clearly than verified welfare outcomes.","The original schedule slipped because of global supply delays."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Egypt’s own social-protection reporting in October 2022 described large spending and poverty-reduction claims.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In observance of the International Day for the Eradication of Poverty, the Ministry of Planning and Economic Development issues a report on social protection and poverty reduction efforts","publisher":"Ministry of Planning and Economic Development","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://mped.gov.eg/singlenews?id=2481","claim":"The ministry reported EGP 321 billion in social subsidies, grants, and benefits in 2021/22, a target of EGP 356 billion in 2022/23, and said Egypt’s social-protection programs had helped reduce poverty and improve living standards, including in the poorest village clusters reached by Hayah Karima.","locator":"lines 59-66","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific budgetary figures and named social programs from an official economic-planning report, though the poverty effects are self-reported.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 fiscal-year reporting with some lagged outcome claims.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to state policy under Sisi via government budgeting and program rollout; outcome claims are partly mediated by earlier baseline trends.","contrary_evidence":["The report is self-evaluative and not independent.","Some poverty figures lag the target year and may not isolate 2022 effects."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The government said it was expanding Takaful and Karama in late 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM follows up with social solidarity min. efforts to expand social protection programs","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-12-22","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/pm-follows-up-with-social-solidarity-min-efforts-to-expand-social-protection-programs/","claim":"The Prime Minister said the government was expanding the social-protection umbrella by adding an extra one million families to Takaful and Karama, bringing beneficiaries to more than 20 million citizens, and that the program had already reached 4.6 million families.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government summary with precise beneficiary totals, but retrieved as a snippet rather than a fully accessible page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 social-protection action.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to presidential directives executed by the cabinet and social-solidarity ministry; implementation is institutional rather than personal.","contrary_evidence":["Expansion demonstrates reach, not necessarily quality or neutrality.","Beneficiary growth does not by itself prove that coverage kept pace with inflation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The World Bank financed a later 2022 expansion of Egypt’s flagship cash-transfer program.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New World Bank Financing Will Expand and Strengthen Egypt’s Flagship Social Protection Program","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/23/new-world-bank-financing-will-expand-and-strengthen-egypt-s-flagship-social-protection-program","claim":"The World Bank announced US$500 million to expand and strengthen Takaful and Karama; as of June 2022 the program covered 3.69 million households, about 12.84 million people, with 74 percent of cardholders/direct beneficiaries women and 67 percent of cash going to Upper Egypt.","locator":"lines 146-157","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank press release with specific coverage and distribution statistics, directly tied to a government program and the financing it received.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 program coverage and year-end financing.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to state policy under Sisi, with implementation through the social-solidarity ministry and donor co-financing; not all outcomes are solely ruler-controlled.","contrary_evidence":["External financing indicates the program required donor support, so it is not purely a domestic achievement.","Coverage data show reach but not benefit adequacy relative to inflation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Egypt’s 2022 macro shock clearly hurt living standards and public finances.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt Economic Monitor, December 2022: Strengthening Resilience through Fiscal and Education Sectors Reforms","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/egypt/publication/egypt-economic-monitor-fall-2022-strengthening-resilience-through-fiscal-and-education-sectors-reforms","claim":"The World Bank said Egypt was experiencing a spike in inflation and abrupt portfolio outflows, that real incomes would be adversely impacted, that poverty may increase because of inflation, and that budgeted education spending remained at 2.0 percent of GDP in FY2022/23, below FY2021/22.","locator":"lines 39-46","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"A detailed World Bank country monitor with clear line-level claims about inflation, poverty risk, and education spending.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 macro and social-welfare context.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to global shocks, but policy response under Sisi mattered through fiscal, monetary, and education choices.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes the government announced social mitigation packages and reforms.","Some negative effects are driven by external shocks rather than domestic policy alone."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}

- IMF support was explicitly tied to social protection and less distortionary macro policy.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Approves 46-month US$3 billion Extended Arrangement for Egypt","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/12/16/pr22441-egypt-imf-executive-board-approves-46-month-usd3b-extended-arrangement","claim":"The IMF said Egypt’s package would preserve macroeconomic stability, restore buffers, reduce inflation, ensure debt sustainability, and increase social spending and social safety nets to protect the vulnerable while moving toward private-sector-led growth.","locator":"lines 257-271","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear IMF press release with explicit policy prescriptions and social-protection language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 policy response to the target-year shock.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Sisi government’s policy choice to seek and accept IMF-supported adjustment, but the package is joint with external lenders and conditional on implementation.","contrary_evidence":["This is a program design, not proof of delivered welfare gains in 2022.","Austerity-like elements can offset some protection gains if implementation is harsh."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- UNICEF documented short-term education support for refugee and migrant children during 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Learning against all odds","publisher":"UNICEF Egypt","publication_date":"2022-02-16","url":"https://www.unicef.org/egypt/stories/learning-against-all-odds","claim":"UNICEF said its teacher-preparedness package enabled safe school operations and back-to-learning support for refugee community schools, creating safe learning conditions for over 17,528 migrant and refugee children and supporting about 30,000 children to learn online and in person.","locator":"lines 67-79","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UNICEF country story with specific beneficiary counts and clearly described interventions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 education and protection activity.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable only in part to government cooperation; UNICEF and donors implemented much of the intervention, but it operated with state school access and consent.","contrary_evidence":["The article is programmatic and does not measure national learning outcomes.","The need for this intervention itself shows existing disruption and vulnerability."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9"]}

- UNICEF also described a 2022 inclusion program for refugee and migrant children.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Learning as a driver for social cohesion","publisher":"UNICEF Egypt","publication_date":"2022-06-20","url":"https://www.unicef.org/egypt/stories/learning-driver-social-cohesion","claim":"UNICEF said a 2021-initiated program aimed to create an inclusive learning environment for refugee and migrant children in Egypt, and that over 3,534 students had benefited from a more inclusive and enabling environment with improved quality and relevance of education.","locator":"lines 67-70","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific UNICEF program description with beneficiary count and direct relevance to vulnerable-group education access.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Program active in 2022, though launched in 2021.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Egyptian education system’s willingness to permit and host the program; the delivery itself is largely UNICEF/CARE driven.","contrary_evidence":["The program serves a limited subset of children, not the whole system.","It is still a targeted intervention rather than a broad national reform outcome."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.9"]}

- Refugees’ access to school and health care was reported as equal-footing inclusion in late 2021 and still relevant as 2022 baseline.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thanks to Egypt, Thousands of Refugees are able to Access Health and Education Services","publisher":"UNHCR Egypt","publication_date":"2021-12-14","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/eg/news/thanks-egypt-thousands-refugees-are-able-access-health-and-education-services","claim":"UNHCR said Egypt reiterated its commitment to maintain and expand services for refugees and asylum-seekers in health and education, that refugee and asylum-seeker students with expired documents could still enroll in public schools, and that they were included in COVID response and vaccination plans on equal footing with Egyptians.","locator":"lines 123-141","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNHCR country office report with concrete policy details and explicit government commitment, though not a government source itself.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2021 baseline that carried into the 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Egyptian government’s cooperation and legal admissions policy; UNHCR and partners implemented and documented the access channels.","contrary_evidence":["This does not prove nationwide service quality or equal treatment in every locality.","It shows access policy, not full outcome equality."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.9"]}

- UNHCR also measured access barriers among refugees in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Health access and utilization survey among refugees in Egypt - 2022","publisher":"UNHCR Data Portal","publication_date":"2022-11-30","url":"https://data.unhcr.org/en/documents/details/109001","claim":"UNHCR said its 2022 cross-sectional survey monitored access to and utilization of key health services among refugees living in Egypt in order to identify barriers encountered.","locator":"lines 14-29","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UNHCR documentation portal with clear description of the survey purpose and target population, though not the survey findings themselves.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 measurement activity.","ruler_attribution":"This is mainly a monitoring instrument; it supports attribution analysis by showing that refugee health access was being actively tracked, not merely presumed.","contrary_evidence":["The page does not report findings, so it cannot by itself establish whether access improved.","It is stronger as evidence of measurement than of outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.6"]}

- Climate and poverty pressures fell hardest on poorer children.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Children's Climate Risk Index: Egypt Report","publisher":"UNICEF Egypt","publication_date":"2022-10-30","url":"https://www.unicef.org/egypt/reports/childrens-climate-risk-index-egypt-report","claim":"UNICEF said the effects of climate change and environmental degradation hit children hardest, especially those living in poorer communities, because they are more exposed and have less capacity to respond.","locator":"line 62","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country report with a direct inequality claim, though it is framed as a general vulnerability assessment rather than a ruler-specific outcome study.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 vulnerability context.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual rather than directly attributable; it describes structural vulnerability that Sisi’s policies may mitigate or aggravate.","contrary_evidence":["The report is about structural risk, not direct policy delivery.","It does not isolate 2022 government performance."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- A later UN/UNICEF/OPHI report gives a 2022 poverty snapshot with strong rural disadvantage.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Multidimensional poverty in Egypt: an in-depth analysis","publisher":"UNICEF Egypt","publication_date":"2024-12-15","url":"https://www.unicef.org/egypt/reports/multidimensional-poverty-egypt-depth-analysis","claim":"The report says multidimensional poverty affected 21 percent of Egyptians in 2022, that poverty was much higher in rural than urban areas, and that services and employment were the biggest contributors to overall deprivation.","locator":"highlight bullets in the report landing page","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Retrospective publication using 2022 data from an inter-agency report; the 2022 figures are clear even though the publication is later.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2022 data point.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the 2022 social and economic environment under Sisi, but the report does not separate policy from inherited or global shocks.","contrary_evidence":["This is retrospective and not a direct contemporaneous 2022 administrative record.","It measures deprivation, not the exact causal share of Sisi’s policy."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across World Bank, IMF, UNICEF, UNHCR, WFP, UNDP, Egyptian ministries, and Egyptian official portals.
- I kept accessible, Egypt-specific sources with concrete 2022 claims: MPED budget and poverty reports, the Cairo governorate Decent Life item, the World Bank’s year-end monitor and TKP financing release, IMF’s EFF approval, UNICEF refugee-learning stories, UNHCR access documents, and the UNICEF climate-poverty pages.
- I rejected several items as duplicates or too thin: index/list pages with no substantive Egypt-2022 claim, current overview pages that were too untethered from the target year, and mirror pages that repeated the same facts without adding independent substance.
- I also rejected or de-prioritized blocked pages: the WFP Egypt annual report download was blocked by 403, and one UN/WHO resource page was mostly metadata with no substantive Egypt-2022 claim.
- I set aside a few post-2022 pages that were only useful as discovery and not needed once 2022-specific evidence was available.
- I kept a mix of supportive and contrary evidence rather than padding with only official positives. The negative side is mainly inflation, poverty risk, education underfunding, and rural/poor-community deprivation.

Remaining gaps:
- Direct, independently measured 2022 household welfare outcomes are still thin, especially for consumption, food security, nutrition, and service quality by region.
- I still lack strong 2022 microdata on whether cash transfers, pensions, and subsidies offset inflation well enough for ordinary households.
- I do not yet have robust independent 2022 evidence on whether social benefits were ever used punitively or conditionally for political loyalty.
- I have some refugee and vulnerable-group coverage, but less on disability access, maternal health quality, and subnational variation outside the better-documented rural/Upper Egypt programs.
- More search would help if you want stronger causal attribution for 2022 life-chance change rather than a policy-and-context dossier.
